CORE
main.c
--unwind 2 --rounds 3 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --no-assertions --datarace
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
A legal re-acquisition of a recursive mutex pruned the path.

`pthread_mutex_lock` has two branches. The one that understands recursion,

    __CPROVER_assert(__CPROVER_get_must(mutex, "mutex-recursive") ||
                     !__CPROVER_get_may(mutex, "mutex-locked"), ...)

sits behind __CPROVER_CUSTOM_BITVECTOR_ANALYSIS and is not compiled for
verification. The branch that is compiled did

    __CPROVER_assume(!*((__CPROVER_mutex_t *)mutex));

which is simply false when the calling thread already holds the mutex. On a
PTHREAD_MUTEX_RECURSIVE mutex that acquisition is legal, so an assumption
that should block a thread instead made every statement after it
unreachable. `pthread_mutexattr_settype` and `pthread_mutex_init` did record
the recursive attribute, but only for the branch that is not compiled.

The effect is vacuity, not a wrong rule: the race below cannot be seen
because main never reaches it, and the SUCCESSFUL that comes back is not a
proof of anything. The same shape as the pthread_exit collapse -- an
unsatisfiable assumption that empties the formula and looks like a result.

Telling a legal re-acquisition from one that would deadlock needs to know who
holds the lock, so the model now carries an owner and a count at the front of
the object. `count` keeps offset 0 and its old encoding -- 0 free, -1
destroyed -- so every existing test on it reads the same value, and a
non-recursive mutex still only ever holds 0 or 1. `pthread_mutexattr_init`
had no model at all, which left the attribute uninitialised; without it the
recursive flag read back would be nondeterministic, and a mutex wrongly
believed recursive stops excluding and invents races.

Measured on `pthread-locked-creation`, the family the 2026 benchmark set
adds: 34 task-property pairs went from -87 points with 4 wrong answers to
the full +45 with none. The four it fixed are exactly the four whose names
carry `recursive`. Controls: a counter guarded by an ordinary mutex, and one
guarded by a recursive mutex locked twice, both stay SUCCESSFUL -- mutual
exclusion is intact.

This is stock CBMC behaviour, not specific to this fork.
